1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:09,295
Your PC takes two full minutes to become usable after you log in, and everyone online gives the same advice: buy more RAM.

2
00:00:09,575 --> 00:00:18,212
In the next few minutes, we'll use one free tool that's already on your PC to find out what's really slowing it down, before you spend anything.

3
00:00:18,492 --> 00:00:23,941
Picture a five year old desktop with 8 gigabytes of RAM and a hard drive.

4
00:00:24,221 --> 00:00:28,767
The desktop appears, but every click takes seconds, and the browser crawls.

5
00:00:29,047 --> 00:00:31,854
Let's diagnose it, one reading at a time.

6
00:00:32,604 --> 00:00:38,464
Press Control, Shift and Escape to open Task Manager, and stay on the Processes tab.

7
00:00:38,744 --> 00:00:44,667
Across the top, you'll see columns for CPU, Memory, Disk, Network and GPU.

8
00:00:44,947 --> 00:00:48,903
Each one is a part of your PC that can run out of capacity.

9
00:00:49,183 --> 00:00:55,457
Click a column header, and the list sorts by that column, so the heaviest process jumps to the top.

10
00:00:55,737 --> 00:00:58,314
What we're hunting for is called a bottleneck.

11
00:00:58,594 --> 00:01:05,959
A computer is a chain of parts handing work to each other, and the whole chain only moves as fast as its slowest link.

12
00:01:06,239 --> 00:01:08,916
Speed up any other link, and nothing changes.

13
00:01:09,666 --> 00:01:10,857
So which link is it?

14
00:01:11,137 --> 00:01:15,857
On our slow desktop, the CPU column reads around 15 percent.

15
00:01:16,137 --> 00:01:18,065
Memory says 68 percent.

16
00:01:18,345 --> 00:01:20,578
And Disk is pinned at 100 percent.

17
00:01:20,858 --> 00:01:25,642
That memory number scares a lot of people, but it isn't a problem by itself.

18
00:01:25,922 --> 00:01:29,479
RAM only slows you down when it actually runs out.

19
00:01:29,759 --> 00:01:31,266
A busy desk is fine.

20
00:01:31,546 --> 00:01:33,551
A desk with no room left is not.

21
00:01:33,831 --> 00:01:35,957
The number that matters here is the disk.

22
00:01:36,707 --> 00:01:39,108
Here's the catch with that 100 percent.

23
00:01:39,388 --> 00:01:41,248
It doesn't mean your drive is full.

24
00:01:41,528 --> 00:01:46,533
It means the drive was busy for the entire time Task Manager was measuring.

25
00:01:46,813 --> 00:01:48,391
That's called active time.

26
00:01:48,671 --> 00:01:53,066
Every request that arrives while the drive is busy has to wait in line.

27
00:01:53,346 --> 00:02:01,355
Open the Performance tab and click the disk, and you'll see the average response time, how long each request takes to be answered.

28
00:02:01,635 --> 00:02:08,757
On a struggling drive, that number climbs as the line grows, and every app that needs a file waits at the back of it.

29
00:02:09,507 --> 00:02:12,339
Why does a hard drive fall behind so easily?

30
00:02:12,619 --> 00:02:17,044
It stores data on spinning platters, read by a head on a moving arm.

31
00:02:17,324 --> 00:02:25,386
For every small, scattered read, the arm has to swing to the right track, which typically takes around 8 to 12 milliseconds.

32
00:02:25,666 --> 00:02:33,501
Then it waits for the platter to spin the data underneath it, about 4 more milliseconds at 7,200 RPM.

33
00:02:33,781 --> 00:02:44,178
That's roughly 14 milliseconds per read, which limits a typical desktop hard drive to somewhere around 75 to 100 random reads per second.

34
00:02:44,458 --> 00:02:47,228
A solid state drive has no arm to move.

35
00:02:47,508 --> 00:02:53,279
It answers in well under a millisecond, and it can handle tens of thousands of reads per second.

36
00:02:54,029 --> 00:02:55,906
Now, who's doing all that reading?

37
00:02:56,186 --> 00:02:58,241
Sort the Processes tab by Disk.

38
00:02:58,521 --> 00:03:09,041
Right after login, a slow PC is often buried in apps that launch themselves at startup, like chat apps, game launchers, updaters and cloud sync.

39
00:03:09,321 --> 00:03:15,490
Each one wants its files at the same moment, and on a hard drive, they all queue up behind each other.

40
00:03:15,770 --> 00:03:17,472
Open the Startup apps tab.

41
00:03:17,752 --> 00:03:20,608
Task Manager rates each app's startup impact.

42
00:03:20,888 --> 00:03:28,210
High means it used more than one second of CPU time, or more than 3 megabytes of disk, while starting.

43
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:32,018
Select the ones you don't need right away, and click Disable.

44
00:03:32,298 --> 00:03:36,799
It costs nothing, and it can take a real bite out of that two minute wait.

45
00:03:37,549 --> 00:03:38,566
But hold on.

46
00:03:38,846 --> 00:03:41,703
Couldn't the disk be busy because RAM is full?

47
00:03:41,983 --> 00:03:46,134
Sometimes, yes, and this is where the RAM advice comes from.

48
00:03:46,414 --> 00:03:55,579
When memory runs short, Windows first compresses data it hasn't touched in a while, typically to around 40 percent of its original size.

49
00:03:55,859 --> 00:04:04,289
If that isn't enough, it moves that data out to the page file, a hidden file on your drive that works as overflow for RAM.

50
00:04:04,569 --> 00:04:08,812
When an app needs the data back, it has to be read from the disk again.

51
00:04:09,092 --> 00:04:14,091
So running out of RAM can show up as a disk stuck at 100 percent.

52
00:04:14,371 --> 00:04:18,699
On a hard drive, that feels like the whole PC is wading through mud.

53
00:04:19,449 --> 00:04:21,291
So how do you tell the two apart?

54
00:04:21,570 --> 00:04:24,204
Go to the Performance tab and click Memory.

55
00:04:24,484 --> 00:04:28,077
Look at In use, and then at Committed, which shows two numbers.

56
00:04:28,357 --> 00:04:33,285
The second one is the commit limit, your RAM and the page file combined.

57
00:04:33,565 --> 00:04:41,886
If In use sits close to your total RAM, and the committed number keeps pushing toward its limit, you really are short on memory.

58
00:04:42,166 --> 00:04:47,821
On our desktop, In use is 5.4 gigabytes out of 8, with room to spare.

59
00:04:48,101 --> 00:04:51,178
So more RAM wouldn't touch the real problem.

60
00:04:51,458 --> 00:04:58,018
The disk is busy because startup apps are queuing on a slow drive, not because memory is overflowing.

61
00:04:58,768 --> 00:05:01,202
That doesn't mean RAM never matters.

62
00:05:01,482 --> 00:05:15,470
HP's current buying guide calls 16 gigabytes the ideal amount for a smooth Windows 11 experience, and says 8 gigabytes can struggle once lots of browser tabs and background apps are open.

63
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:19,496
If you regularly fill your RAM, more of it helps a lot.

64
00:05:19,776 --> 00:05:26,440
If you don't, the extra RAM just sits there empty, and empty RAM doesn't make anything faster.

65
00:05:27,190 --> 00:05:29,890
Two other culprits are worth a check while you're here.

66
00:05:30,170 --> 00:05:34,547
If the CPU column stays near 100 percent, sort by it.

67
00:05:34,827 --> 00:05:42,258
One runaway process might be hogging the processor, or the chip might simply be too slow for what you're asking of it.

68
00:05:42,538 --> 00:05:49,267
And if your PC feels fine at first but slows down after a few minutes of heavy work, heat might be the brake.

69
00:05:49,547 --> 00:05:56,661
Most processors have a maximum temperature somewhere between 95 and 110 degrees Celsius.

70
00:05:56,941 --> 00:06:01,114
When they reach it, they cut power and clock speed to protect themselves.

71
00:06:01,394 --> 00:06:02,826
That's thermal throttling.

72
00:06:03,106 --> 00:06:07,446
On the Performance tab, the CPU page shows its current speed.

73
00:06:07,726 --> 00:06:13,206
If that speed sinks under load while the fans roar, clean out the dust and check your cooling.

74
00:06:13,956 --> 00:06:15,836
Gamers, there's one more column.

75
00:06:16,116 --> 00:06:21,073
In a demanding game, a GPU running near 99 percent is normal.

76
00:06:21,353 --> 00:06:25,980
It means the graphics card is the limit, which is exactly what you paid for.

77
00:06:26,260 --> 00:06:36,115
But if the GPU sits well below that while the CPU works flat out, the graphics card is waiting for instructions that can't arrive fast enough.

78
00:06:36,395 --> 00:06:40,899
That's a CPU bottleneck, and a faster graphics card won't fix it.

79
00:06:41,649 --> 00:06:42,960
Back to our desktop.

80
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:54,992
With the startup apps trimmed, it settles down sooner, but the real bottleneck is still that hard drive, and no amount of RAM changes how fast its arm can move.

81
00:06:55,272 --> 00:06:59,118
The fix that matters is moving Windows onto an SSD.

82
00:06:59,398 --> 00:07:01,414
And this method works on any PC.

83
00:07:01,694 --> 00:07:07,866
Open Task Manager, sort each column, find the part that's pinned at the top, and fix that part.

84
00:07:08,146 --> 00:07:09,928
Not the part the internet guessed.

85
00:07:10,678 --> 00:07:12,734
Next time, we'll dig into that fix.

86
00:07:13,014 --> 00:07:22,785
SSD versus HDD versus NVMe: what really makes games load faster, and when a faster drive stops making a difference.

87
00:07:23,065 --> 00:07:27,022
If this helped you find your bottleneck, subscribe, so you don't miss it.

